<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day17" xml:id="day-17-lecture">
    <title>Day 17</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 6.2: Shrinkage - The Lasso
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>What is regularization? Why do we need it?</li>
            <li>
                What are the two basic types of regularization methods?
                How are they implemented mathematically in linear regression?
                Why are they also called shrinkage methods?
            </li>

            <li>How do you fit a Lasso regression model in Python?</li>
            <li>
                How do you control model flexibility and the bias-variance
                tradeoff when using regularization?
            </li>

            <li>
                How do you find the right amount of regularization using
                cross-validation?
                How do you do this in Python?
            </li>

            <li>
                What additional precautions do you need to take when using
                regularization compared with least squares?
            </li>

            <li>When do you choose one shrinkage method over another?</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>
           Lasso: Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator
        </title>

        <p>
            Same goal as before:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Fit model using all <m>p</m> predictors.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Aim to constrain (regularize) coefficient estimates.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Shrink the coefficient estimates towards 0.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Ridge shrinks coefficients but does not eliminate them, which is
                what we actually want.
            </p>
        </note>

        <p>
            <md>
                Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \beta_2 X_2 + \beta_3 X_3 + \beta_4 X_4
            </md>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>The lasso</title>

        <p>
            Least Squares:
        </p>

        <p>
            <md>
                RSS = \sum_{i=1}^{n}
      \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Ridge:
        </p>

        <p>
            <md>
                \sum_{i=1}^{n}
      \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
      + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2
      = RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            The Lasso:
        </p>

        <p>
            <md>
                \sum_{i=1}^{n}
      \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
      + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
      = RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\ell_1</m> penalty instead of <m>\ell_2</m> penalty
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\ell_1</m> norm: <m>\|\beta\|_1 = \sum |\beta_j|</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\ell_2</m> norm: <m>\|\beta\|_2 = \sum \beta_j^2</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\ell_p</m> norm:
                        <m>\|\beta\|_p = (\sum |\beta_j|^p)^{1/p}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\ell_\infty</m> norm:
                        <m>\|\beta\|_\infty = \max\{|\beta_i|\}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <example>
        <introduction>
                <p>
                    Each square in the shown figure has sides of unit length. For each of the following points, compute the <m>\ell_1</m>, <m>\ell_2</m>, and <m>\ell_\infty</m> norms.
                </p>
                <image source="figures/ell_norm_class_example.png" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>A 2D x-y grid showing 4 points labeled as a, b, c, and d. Each point is in one of the quadrants.</shortdescription>
                    
                </image>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Point a=(3,3)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>
                    <mrow>\ell_1 \amp= |3|+|3|=6</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_2 \amp= \sqrt{3^2+3^2}=\sqrt{18}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_\infty \amp= \max\{3,3\}=3</mrow>                        
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
                <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Point b=(-4,3)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>
                    <mrow>\ell_1 \amp= |-4|+|3|=4+3=7</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_2 \amp= \sqrt{(-4)^2+3^2}=\sqrt{25}=5</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_\infty \amp= \max\{4,3\}=4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
                <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Point c=(-1,-2)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>
                    <mrow>\ell_1 \amp= |-1|+|-2|=1+2=3</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_2 \amp= \sqrt{(-1)^2+(-2)^2}=\sqrt{5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_\infty \amp= \max\{1,2\}=2</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
                <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Point d=(4,-4)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>
                    <mrow>\ell_1 \amp= |4|+|-4|=4+4=8</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_2 \amp= \sqrt{4^2+(-4)^2}=\sqrt{32}=4\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\ell_\infty \amp= \max\{4,4\}=4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>

        </example>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Subsets with lasso</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                \sum_{i=1}^{n}
      \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
      + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
      = RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The result of the <m>\ell_1</m> penalty is that it
                        forces some coefficients to be exactly 0.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This can be interpreted as something like subset
                        selection, but it is called <em>variable selection</em>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The result is <em>sparse</em> models, which use only a
                        subset of variables.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Selecting a good <m>\lambda</m> is critical.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>An example on the <c>Credit</c> data set</title>

        <sidebyside widths="47% 47%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    Lasso
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig6-6a.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Plot of lasso coefficient paths for the Credit data,
                        showing how some coefficient estimates shrink exactly to
                        zero as lambda increases.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Ridge
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig6-4a.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Plot of ridge regression coefficient paths for the
                        Credit data, showing how the coefficient estimates
                        shrink toward zero as lambda increases.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\lambda = 0</m>: Both are least squares.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\lambda = \infty</m>: All coefficients are 0.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Only lasso has coefficients equal to 0 at finite
                        <m>\lambda</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>More example on the Credit data set</title>

        <sidebyside widths="47% 47%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    Lasso
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig6-6b.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Plot of lasso coefficient paths for the Credit data,
                        showing how some coefficient estimates shrink exactly to
                        zero and produce variable subsets.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Ridge
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig6-4b.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Plot of ridge regression coefficient paths for the
                        Credit data, showing how coefficient estimates shrink as
                        the relative L2 norm decreases from left to right.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Note that ridge always includes all variables, even if a
                        coefficient is small.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Lasso produces different-sized subsets depending on the
                        amount of regularization.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Why can lasso select variables while ridge cannot?</title>

  <p>
    Alternative formulation of lasso and ridge regression    
  </p>
  
      <figure xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal">
        <caption>Playing with different values of <m>p</m>.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="p-norm-unit-ball" geogebra="euymx5b9" width="90%" aspect="16:9" reset-icon="yes">
        <shortdescription>
            An interactive Geogebra applet showing the unit ball for different values of p. The user can change the value of p using a slider, and see how the shape of the unit ball changes.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
            <p>
                An interactive Geogebra applet showing the unit ball in <m>\ell_p</m> norm for different values of <m>p</m>. The user can change the value of <m>p</m> using a slider, and see how the shape of the unit ball changes. 
            </p>
        </description>
        </interactive>
    </figure>
   

  <p>
    <md>
      \min_{\beta} \sum (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2
      \quad \text{where} \quad \sum |\beta_j| \leq s
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
      \min_{\beta} \sum (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2
      \quad \text{where} \quad \sum |\beta_j|^2 \leq s
    </md>
  </p>

  <image source="figures/lasso_ridge_contour.png" width="70%">
    <shortdescription>Contour plot illustrating the geometric difference between lasso and ridge constraints, showing why lasso can set coefficients exactly to zero while ridge generally cannot.</shortdescription>
    <description><p><m>\hat{\beta}</m> is the least squares solution. Each of the ellipses represents parameter combinations that yield a constant value of the sum of squared residuals. The blue region is the <m>p</m>-norm ball: diamond for <m>p=1</m> and circle for <m>p=2</m>. The parameter estimates that minimize the objective function are at the point where a contour first intersects the boundary of the <m>p</m>-norm ball.</p></description>
  </image>
  <p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                The ridge constraint region is circular, so the contours of the least squares error function are more likely to intersect the constraint region at a point where both coefficients are nonzero.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                The lasso constraint region is diamond-shaped, so the contours of the least squares error function are more likely to intersect the constraint region at a corner, where one of the coefficients is zero.
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            In higher dimensions, the lasso constraint region gets more and more spiky with many corners, and many of the coefficients can simultaneously be set to zero.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Consider the shown RSS contours and the lasso and ridge constraint regions. <m>\hat{\beta}</m> is the least square solution. Each square in the grid has unit length. 
        </p>
            <image source="figures/pictorial_optimization_soln.png" width="75%">
            <shortdescription>Four subplots each showing two elliptical contours centered around the least squares solution. The top two panels show diamonds (p=1 norm) at the origin. The bottom two show circles (p=1 norm) at the origin. </shortdescription>
            </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the optimal parameter combination for (a)?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>(\beta_1, \beta_2)=(-1,-1)</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the optimal parameter combination for (b)?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>(\beta_1, \beta_2)=(0,2)</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the optimal parameter combination for (c)?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>(\beta_1, \beta_2)=(0.5,-1.9)</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the optimal parameter combination for (d)?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>(\beta_1, \beta_2)=(-2,2)</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
  </exercise>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Bias-Variance tradeoff</title>

  <image source="figures/Fig6-5.png" width="90%">
    <shortdescription>Plot illustrating the bias-variance tradeoff for simulated data, showing squared bias, variance, and test mean squared error.</shortdescription>
  </image>

  <p>Squared bias (black), variance (green), and test mean squared error (purple) for simulated data.</p>

    <p>The horizontal dashed line is the minimum possible test MSE.</p>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Using Cross-Validation to find <m>\lambda</m></title>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Choose a grid of <m>\lambda</m> values.</p></li>
      <li><p>Compute the (<m>k</m>-fold) cross-validation error for each value of <m>\lambda</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Select the tuning parameter value <m>\lambda</m> for which the CV error is smallest.</p></li>
      <li><p>Refit the model using all available observations and the selected tuning parameter value.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>10-fold CV choice of <m>\lambda</m> for lasso and simulated data</title>

  <image source="figures/Fig6-13.png" width="90%">
    <shortdescription>Plot of 10-fold cross-validation error and lasso coefficient behavior for simulated data, with dashed vertical lines indicating the selected lambda and only the signal variables remaining nonzero near the chosen model.</shortdescription>
  </image>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Data generated with <m>p = 45</m> variables and <m>n = 50</m> data points; only 2 variables are actually involved.</p></li>
      <li><p>The vertical dashed line indicates the choice of <m>\lambda</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>On the right, the two colored lines are predictors related to the response (signal); grey lines are unrelated (noise).</p></li>
      <li><p>The lasso correctly gives much larger coefficient estimates to the two signal predictors. The minimum CV error also corresponds to coefficient estimates for which only the signal variables are nonzero.</p></li>
      <li><p>Note that the least squares solution does not even emphasize one of the variables (the green one).</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Ridge vs Lasso</title>

  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p><term>Ridge Regression:</term></p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Coefficients are shrunk towards 0, but are not forced to be exactly 0.</p></li>
          <li><p>Tends to be better than lasso when many predictors have a significant effect on the output.</p></li>
          <li><p>More robust to outliers.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>

    <stack>
      <p><term>Lasso:</term></p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Coefficients are shrunk towards 0, and some are forced to be exactly 0.</p></li>
          <li><p>Tends to be more interpretable because we can see which variables are nonzero, rather than having to decide how small is small enough.</p></li>
          <li><p>More sensitive to outliers.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>TL;DR - Original formulation</title>

      <p><term>Least Squares:</term></p>
      <p>
        <md>
          RSS = \sum_{i=1}^{n}
          \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
        </md>
      </p>

      <p><term>Ridge:</term></p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \sum_{i=1}^{n}
          \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
          + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2
          = RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2
        </md>
      </p>


  <p><term>The Lasso:</term></p>
  <p>
    <md>
      \sum_{i=1}^{n}
      \left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
      + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
      = RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} |\beta_j|
    </md>
  </p>
</paragraphs>


</handout>